    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d463">

                <pb facs="0551" n="471" sameAs="frus:frus1902/pg471"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Hill to Mr. Choate.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1901-04-30"><hi rendition="#italic">April 30,
                        1901</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 620.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I inclose a copy of a letter from Frank
                    Lawrence, from which it appears that Harry Wood, a native American citizen, is
                    now a prisoner <pb facs="0552" n="472" xml:id="pg472"/> of war, held at Ragama
                    Camp, in Ceylon, and is sick with enteric fever and not able well to bear the
                    rigors and restrictions of the life of a prisoner.</p>
                <p>You will inquire whether, in view of Mr. Wood’s health, it may not be possible to
                    release him on parole on condition that he return to the United States, his
                    family furnishing the passage money.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">David J. Hill</hi>,<lb/><hi
                            rendition="#italic">Acting Secretary.</hi></signed></closer>



                <xi:include href="d463-a1.xml" xpointer="d463-a1"/>
